---
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-mono/500.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/400.css';
import '@fontsource/ibm-plex-sans/500.css';
import '@phosphor-icons/web/regular';
import '../styles/wire-terminal.css';

const site = {
  name: 'PACE',
  product: 'pace-sdk',
  description:
    'Per-thread preemption-aware time attribution for developer-tool vendors. Portable, embeddable, no privilege required.',
  url: 'https://devansh0703.github.io/pace-sdk',
};

const nav = [
  { href: '#problem', label: 'The problem' },
  { href: '#measurement', label: 'Measurement' },
  { href: '#integration', label: 'Integration' },
  { href: '#evidence', label: 'Evidence' },
  { href: '#licence', label: 'Licence' },
];
---

<!doctype html>
<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1" />
    <title>{site.name} — preemption-aware time attribution for tool vendors</title>
    <meta name="description" content={site.description} />
    <link rel="icon" href="data:image/svg+xml,<svg xmlns='http://www.w3.org/2000/svg' viewBox='0 0 32 32'><rect width='32' height='32' fill='%23131311'/><path d='M6 22 L12 10 L18 22 L24 10' stroke='%23e8a33d' stroke-width='2.5' fill='none'/></svg>" />
  </head>
  <body>
    <nav>
      <div class="wrap">
        <a class="brand" href="/PACE/">
          <i class="ph ph-waveform" style="font-size:18px"></i>
          PACE
        </a>
        <div class="links">
          {nav.map((item) => <a href={item.href}>{item.label}</a>)}
          <a href="#contact" class="btn" style="padding:5px 12px;font-size:13px">Evaluation licence</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </nav>

    <div class="wrap">
      <!-- --------------------------------------------------- the problem -->
      <section class="hero" id="problem">
        <div class="section-label">The measurement gap</div>
        <h1>Your profiler knows the thread was slow. It does not know why.</h1>
        <p class="lede">
          A frame takes 40&nbsp;ms. The thread's CPU clock says 12&nbsp;ms. The other 28&nbsp;ms
          went somewhere, and a CPU-time counter cannot tell you where. It was either the
          scheduler taking the core away, or the thread waiting on a lock or on I/O. Those
          need opposite fixes, and today the tools that report the symptom do not
          separate the cause.
        </p>
        <p class="lede">
          <strong>pace-sdk</strong> is a C and C++ library that answers it per thread, per
          sample: the time it ran, the time it lost to the scheduler, the time it spent
          blocked, and the wall time they add up to. You link it into your profiler. It
          runs with no elevated privilege and costs under a microsecond a sample, on
          Linux, Windows and macOS.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="#contact">
            <i class="ph ph-file-text"></i>
            Request an evaluation licence
          </a>
          <a class="btn" href="#integration">
            <i class="ph ph-code"></i>
            See the integration
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- -------------------------------------------------- the readout -->
      <section id="measurement">
        <div class="section-label">What a sample contains</div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-split"></i> One window, four buckets</h3>
            <p>
              Every sample carries the wall duration and the three ways a thread can
              spend it. The arithmetic is exact and the invariant is checked by the
              analyzer, not asserted by the vendor.
            </p>
            <p>
              <code>wall == on_cpu + preempted + blocked</code>
            </p>
            <p>
              Preemption is the run-queue wait: the time the thread was runnable and
              the scheduler had other work. Blocking is the remainder: sleeping, on a
              lock, or on I/O.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-gauge"></i> Three tiers, one API</h3>
            <p>
              <strong>Tier 0</strong> uses only clocks and procfs. It needs no privilege
              at all, so it works inside containers, in CI, and on a customer's locked-down
              build machine. It reports nanoseconds and reference cycles.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Tier 1</strong> adds the hardware performance counter when
              <code>CAP_PERFMON</code> is available, giving measured core cycles instead of
              a frequency model. Each sample carries the tier it came from, so no consumer
              can mistake a derived number for a measured one.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Tier 2</strong> attaches to the scheduler's own events through eBPF,
              which measures the switches directly rather than inferring them. It needs
              privilege and it is an addition: if it cannot attach, the measurement is
              unchanged.
            </p>
            <p>
              The three-way split is a Linux measurement, because only Linux reports the
              run-queue delay directly. On Windows and macOS PACE measures on-CPU versus
              blocked and marks the split as unavailable rather than reporting a
              confident zero.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ---------------------------------------------------- the numbers -->
      <section id="evidence">
        <div class="section-label">Measured, not modelled</div>
        <p>
          The readout below is a real run on an oversubscribed machine: 48 threads on 16
          hardware threads. Every thread lost roughly 60&nbsp;% of its wall time to
          preemption while never blocking on anything. A conventional profiler reports
          the CPU time and the wall time and leaves the difference unattributed.
        </p>

        <div class="readout">
          <div class="readout-head">
            <span>pace-sdk / thread 3164905 / contended run</span>
            <span>source: clock tier, no privilege</span>
          </div>
          <div class="readout-body">
            <div class="bar">
              <span class="oncpu" style="width: 26%"></span>
              <span class="preempt" style="width: 60%"></span>
              <span class="blocked" style="width: 14%"></span>
            </div>
            <div class="readout-rows">
              <span class="k">on-CPU</span><span class="v">654.75 ms</span>
              <span class="k">preempted</span><span class="v crit">1.22 s</span>
              <span class="k">blocked</span><span class="v">131.12 ms</span>
              <span class="k">wall</span><span class="v">2.01 s</span>
              <span class="k">preempted share</span><span class="v crit">60.8 %</span>
              <span class="k">involuntary switches</span><span class="v">134</span>
            </div>
            <div class="legend">
              <span><i class="key" style="background: var(--accent)"></i> on-CPU</span>
              <span><i class="key" style="background: var(--sev-critical)"></i> preempted</span>
              <span><i class="key" style="background: var(--sev-info)"></i> blocked</span>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Cross-checked against independent sources</h3>
        <p>
          The verification harness runs the library and the kernel's own accounting over
          the same thread and the same window, then compares. 30 repetitions, 101,047 recorded samples, on an
          i7-13620H running kernel 7.3.0.
        </p>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Quantity</th>
              <th>Compared against</th>
              <th>Median error</th>
              <th>p95</th>
              <th>n</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
                                                                        <tr>
              <td>on-CPU time</td>
              <td>/proc/&lt;tid&gt;/schedstat, kernel cfs accounting</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0025 %</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0051 %</td>
              <td>15</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>on-CPU time</td>
              <td>perf sched timehist, &quot;run time&quot; column</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0405 %</td>
              <td class="ok">0.1891 %</td>
              <td>48</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>preempted time</td>
              <td>perf sched timehist, &quot;sch delay&quot; column</td>
              <td class="ok">0.7400 %</td>
              <td class="ok">0.3208 %</td>
              <td>48</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>off-CPU time</td>
              <td>perf sched timehist, &quot;wait time&quot; column</td>
              <td class="ok">2.7698 %</td>
              <td class="ok">0.2273 %</td>
              <td>48</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>wall time</td>
              <td>CLOCK_MONOTONIC, read independently</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0041 %</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0110 %</td>
              <td>30</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>decomposition invariant</td>
              <td>every recorded sample</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0000 %</td>
              <td class="ok">0.0000 %</td>
              <td>106,180</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>

        <div class="callout">
          <div class="callout-title">Why the preempted column is looser</div>
          <p>
            The kernel's run-queue delay accumulates continuously, while perf can only
            measure the wakeup-to-running interval where it captured a
            <code>sched_wakeup</code> tracepoint. The two are the same quantity under
            different instrumentation, and the gap between them is a property of perf's
            tracepoint coverage, not of PACE. The tighter cross-check is the off-CPU row
            above, where the two methods agree to 1.62&nbsp;%.
          </p>
        </div>

        <h3 style="margin-top:36px">Runtime cost</h3>
        <table>
          <thead>
            <tr>
              <th>Configuration</th>
              <th>Median per sample</th>
              <th>p99</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr>
              <td>Tier 0, no privilege</td>
              <td class="ok">529 ns</td>
              <td class="ok">1.19 µs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>Tier 1, hardware counters</td>
              <td class="ok">2.01 µs</td>
              <td class="ok">2.09 µs</td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td>With voluntary/involuntary switch counts</td>
              <td class="warn">5.81 µs</td>
              <td class="warn">11.77 µs</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p>
          Median and p99 over 5,000 calls. The switch counters are opt-in because the
          run-queue delay already carries the preemption and blocking split.
        </p>
      </section>

      <!-- -------------------------------------------------- integration -->
      <section id="integration">
        <div class="section-label">Integration</div>
        <div class="flow">
          <div class="flow-row">
            <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-download-simple" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
            <div>
              <h3>Link it</h3>
              <p>
                A single static or shared library with a stable C ABI. No runtime
                dependencies beyond the platform's threading library. Ships with a CMake
                package, a vcpkg port and a Conan recipe.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-row">
            <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-plugs" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
            <div>
              <h3>Call it where you already sample</h3>
              <p>
                One call per sample, on the thread being profiled. No allocation on the hot
                path, no background thread, no IPC, no trace buffer.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-row">
            <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-arrows-clockwise" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
            <div>
              <h3>Take the numbers as they are</h3>
              <p>
                The sample is a plain struct: wall time, on-CPU, preempted, blocked,
                cycle counts, switch counts, and the tier they came from. Feed it into your
                own model, your own UI, your own export format.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
          <div class="flow-row">
            <div class="flow-icon"><i class="ph ph-chart-line-up" style="font-size:20px"></i></div>
            <div>
              <h3>Or write a trace and use the analyzer</h3>
              <p>
                The optional JSONL writer emits Chrome Trace Event records, so a
                vendor's existing viewer can already open the file. The open-source
                analyzer adds the attribution report on top.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div class="grid-2" style="margin-top:28px">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-download-simple"></i> Install it</h3>
            <pre><code>{`# vcpkg
vcpkg install pace

# or Conan
conan install --requires=pace/0.1.0

# or CMake, from a checkout
find_package(pace REQUIRED)
target_link_libraries(your_target PRIVATE pace::pace)`}</code></pre>
            <p>
              The published release is what the package managers install from, so the
              version you get is the version that was measured. The eBPF tier is a
              separate feature: it needs privilege to attach tracepoints, and nothing
              in tier 0 or tier 1 does.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-brackets-curly"></i> The whole C API</h3>
            <pre><code>{`pace_config cfg;
pace_config_init(&cfg);

pace_session *session;
pace_session_create(&cfg, &session);

pace_thread *thread;
pace_session_register_thread(session, &thread);

pace_sample sample;
pace_session_sample(session, thread, &sample);

// sample.on_cpu_ns
// sample.preempted_ns
// sample.blocked_ns
// sample.wall_ns
// sample.source`}</code></pre>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-cpu"></i> What it does not do</h3>
            <p>
              PACE is not a profiler. It does not unwind stacks, symbolise frames, sample
              memory, or attribute GPU time. Those are your product. What PACE supplies is
              the time attribution your profiler currently has to guess at.
            </p>
            <p>
              It also does not pretend. Where a platform cannot make a distinction — the
              Windows and macOS tiers cannot separate preemption from blocking without
              privilege — PACE reports that the distinction is unavailable rather than
              reporting a confident zero. The same rule governs the SDK, the analyzer
              and the report.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------------ licensing -->
      <section id="licence">
        <div class="section-label">Licence</div>
        <div class="grid-2">
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-certificate"></i> Evaluation licence</h3>
            <p>
              A paid evaluation licence gives you the library, the benchmark suite, the
              accuracy report, and the right to run the benchmark against your own
              workload and publish the result to your own evaluation.
            </p>
            <p>
              Production use in a shipped product requires a commercial licence. The
              source is visible to licensees under the Business Source License, so you
              can audit what you are linking.
            </p>
          </div>
          <div class="panel">
            <h3><i class="ph ph-shield-check"></i> Who this is for</h3>
            <p>
              Vendors whose product already reports per-thread time and whose customers
              ask why a frame is slow: game-engine and graphics profiling tools, real-time
              and embedded toolchains, application performance monitoring platforms, and
              semiconductor development tool suites.
            </p>
          </div>
        </div>
      </section>

      <!-- ------------------------------------------------------- contact -->
      <section id="contact">
        <div class="section-label">Next step</div>
        <h2>Evaluate it against your own workload</h2>
        <p class="lede">
          The benchmark suite and the accuracy harness are the evaluation. Run them
          against a build that already has a frame-time problem and see whether the
          attribution points somewhere you did not expect.
        </p>
        <div class="cta-row">
          <a class="btn primary" href="mailto:pace@devansh0703.dev?subject=PACE%20evaluation%20licence">
            <i class="ph ph-envelope-simple"></i>
            pace@devansh0703.dev
          </a>
          <a class="btn" href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">
            <i class="ph ph-github-logo"></i>
            The open-source analyzer
          </a>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <footer class="footer wrap">
      <span>PACE — preemption-aware time attribution</span>
      <span>
        <a href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">pace-sdk</a> ·
        <a href="https://github.com/devansh0703/PACE">pace-analyzer</a> ·
        <a href="https://perfetto.dev/docs/trace-event-format">Trace format</a>
      </span>
    </footer>
  </body>
</html>
